---
/**
 * Why conventional primary care usually cannot deliver the experience the
 * hero describes, beside the dot field: 2,500 dots, one per patient on a
 * typical panel. On reveal the forest fill drains from the bottom in 45 steps
 * over 2.2s while the numeral counts down from 2,500 to 250; then the sage
 * hairline and the explanatory line fade in. Reduced motion renders the end
 * state with no movement.
 *
 * Copy rule for this section: the constraint is structural, never personal.
 * It is about panel math and reimbursement, not about bad physicians or a
 * named health system. Keep it that way if this copy is ever revised.
 */
---

<section class="problem" aria-label="Why this is hard to find">
  <div class="rd-wrap problem-grid">
    <div class="problem-text">
      <h2 class="rd-h2 problem-h2">Most doctors want to give you this. The system rarely lets them.</h2>
      <p>
        A typical primary care physician is responsible for two to three thousand
        patients. To keep a practice solvent under insurance reimbursement, that
        schedule has to move in ten and fifteen minute increments, which means the
        visit routinely ends before the conversation does. That is not a failure of
        skill or of caring. It is what the arithmetic of the model requires.
      </p>
      <p>
        The downstream effects are familiar to almost everyone. You wait weeks for an
        appointment, then explain your history to someone reading it for the first
        time. Your questions get routed through a portal or a front desk. Care gets
        divided among specialists who never quite talk to one another, and no single
        physician is holding the whole picture. Prevention, which takes time and
        repetition, becomes whatever fits in the minutes that are left.
      </p>
      <p>
        None of this is fixable one visit at a time. It is fixable by changing the
        arithmetic. Fewer patients per physician, visits long enough to finish the
        conversation, and a practice funded directly by the people it cares for
        rather than by billing volume. That is the entire idea behind Forward Family
        Medicine.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="dotfield" data-reveal="0.4">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 500 500" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="A field of 2,500 dots, one per patient on a typical physician's panel, with the top 250 shaded forest green to show a Forward Family Medicine panel">
        <defs>
          <pattern id="dotFaint" width="10" height="10" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"><circle cx="5" cy="5" r="2.3" fill="#D8D0C2"></circle></pattern>
          <pattern id="dotForest" width="10" height="10" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"><circle cx="5" cy="5" r="2.3" fill="#1E2D1F"></circle></pattern>
        </defs>
        <rect x="0" y="0" width="500" height="500" fill="url(#dotFaint)"></rect>
        <rect class="dotfill" x="0" y="0" width="500" height="500" fill="url(#dotForest)"></rect>
        <line class="dotline" x1="0" y1="52.5" x2="500" y2="52.5" stroke="#8FAB8E" stroke-width="1"></line>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        <div class="dotnum rd-serif">250</div>
        <div class="dotcap">patients per physician. One dot each.</div>
        <div class="dotafter">The green dots are a Forward Family Medicine panel. The faint ones are the rest of a typical panel of 2,500.</div>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .problem {
    background: var(--linen-50);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--hairline);
    padding: 104px 0;
  }
  .problem-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 72px;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .problem-text { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 22px; }
  .problem-h2 { line-height: 1.12; }
  .problem-text p {
    font-size: 16px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    color: var(--text-900);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .dotfield { margin: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 18px; }
  .dotfield svg { display: block; }
  .dotfield figcaption { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 6px; }
  .dotnum {
    font-size: 84px;
    line-height: 1;
    color: var(--forest);
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .dotcap { font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--forest); font-weight: 500; }
  .dotafter {
    font-size: 14px;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-600);
    margin-top: 6px;
    padding-top: 12px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline-strong);
  }

  /* Motion. Rest state: the forest layer covers everything. On reveal the
     clip drains from the bottom to leave the top 10% (250 of 2,500). */
  .dotfill { clip-path: inset(0 0 0 0); transition: clip-path 2.2s steps(45, end) 0.7s; }
  .dotfield.on .dotfill { clip-path: inset(0 0 90% 0); }
  .dotline { opacity: 0; transition: opacity 0.4s ease 2.9s; }
  .dotfield.on .dotline { opacity: 1; }
  .dotafter { opacity: 0; transition: opacity 0.5s ease 3.1s; }
  .dotfield.on .dotafter { opacity: 1; }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .dotfill { clip-path: inset(0 0 90% 0); }
    .dotline, .dotafter { opacity: 1; }
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .problem { padding: 64px 0; }
    .problem-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 28px; }
    .dotnum { font-size: 56px; }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // The numeral flips to 2,500 on reveal and counts down to 250 in step with
  // the drain (0.7s delay, 2.2s, ease-out). Reduced motion: stays at 250.
  const field = document.querySelector('.dotfield') as HTMLElement | null;
  const num = field?.querySelector('.dotnum') as HTMLElement | null;
  const reduced = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;

  function countDown() {
    if (!num || reduced) return;
    const fmt = (n: number) => Math.round(n).toLocaleString('en-US');
    const delay = 700, dur = 2200, from = 2500, to = 250;
    let start: number | null = null;
    num.textContent = fmt(from);
    const step = (ts: number) => {
      if (start === null) start = ts;
      const t = ts - start - delay;
      if (t < 0) { requestAnimationFrame(step); return; }
      const p = Math.min(t / dur, 1);
      const eased = 1 - Math.pow(1 - p, 3);
      num.textContent = fmt(from + (to - from) * eased);
      if (p < 1) requestAnimationFrame(step); else num.textContent = fmt(to);
    };
    requestAnimationFrame(step);
  }

  field?.addEventListener('reveal', countDown, { once: true });
</script>
